  "solution": {
    "text": "<p>When calcium chloride reacts with sodium carbonate, a double replacement reaction takes place. The products of this reaction are calcium carbonate and sodium chloride.<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>CaCl</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>Na</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi>CO</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#8594;</mo><munder><msub><mi>CaCO</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mfenced><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">X</mi></mfenced></munder><mo>+</mo><munder><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>NaCl</mi></mrow><mfenced><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Y</mi></mfenced></munder></math></p><p>To Obtain Calcium chloride from Calcium carbonate we need to react calcium carbonate with Hydro chloric acid.</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><munder><msub><mi>CaCO</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mfenced><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">X</mi></mfenced></munder><mo>+</mo><munder><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>HCl</mi></mrow><mfenced><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Z</mi></mfenced></munder><mo>&#8594;</mo><msub><mi>CaCl</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>CO</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mfenced><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g</mi></mfenced><mo>+</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mfenced><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mfenced></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>&#8756;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">X</mi><mo>)</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><msub><mi>CaCO</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Y</mi><mo>)</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>NaCl</mi></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Z</mi><mo>)</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>HCl</mi></math></p>",
